## How a PRD records decisions and connections

Every PRD in `docs/product/prds/` follows `TEMPLATE.md`. The rules below are what `m check-prd-links`
enforces; run `m sync-prd-links` after any change and `m check-prd-links` before you finish.

### One PRD owns an area, and it is always current

A PRD's body describes the product **as it is meant to be today**, never as it was first written. When
a later decision changes a requirement, edit that requirement in place in the PRD that owns it. Never
leave a requirement, a Learner experience walk-through, or an Objects section saying something the
product no longer does — a reader must be able to open one PRD and trust all of it.

The document that argued for the change keeps the argument: its Problem, UbD grounding, Learner
experience, and AI behavior sections explain why the change was right. It does not keep the
requirements; those move to the PRD that owns the area.

### Requirement numbers are permanent

Other PRDs, architecture docs, and header fields cite requirements by number. Never renumber.

- A **changed** requirement keeps its number and gets new text.
- A **new** requirement is appended at the end of its subsection, taking the next free number in the
  document.
- A **retired** requirement keeps its number and becomes a one-line stub naming what replaced it:

  ```
  4. **Retired** by PRD 29 §8-14 on 2026-09-12. See the Decision log.
  ```

  The original text lives in git history and the Decision log entry. Never delete the line.

### The header block

Lines 3 onward, in this order, with no other fields:

```
**Status:** Reviewed
**Depends on:** PRD 04 (Learner model structure), PRD 08 (Goal negotiation)
**Depended on by:** PRD 12 (Evidence plan), PRD 21 (Step selection)
**Changes:** PRD 07 (Intent capture) §1, §3-5
**Changed by:** PRD 30 (Course setup repairs) §2, §5, §18-20
**Supersedes:** PRD 23 (Progress visibility) §1-4, §12
**Superseded by:** PRD 29 (Retiring the learner-model browse surfaces) §3-7
```

- `Status` is exactly `Draft`, `Reviewed`, or `Reviewed, deliberately out of v1`. It MUST NOT name
  another PRD — relationships live in the fields below it, which is why the status used to drift.
- `Depends on`, `Changes` and `Supersedes` are written by hand. They are the authoritative edges.
- `Depended on by`, `Changed by` and `Superseded by` are **generated**. Never hand-edit them; run
  `m sync-prd-links`.
- `Status`, `Depends on` and `Depended on by` are always present. The other four appear only when
  non-empty, and sync adds and removes them.
- `Changes` means "this PRD rewrote those requirements, which still live in the PRD that owns them".
  `Supersedes` means "those requirements are retired; this PRD took over the area".
- Every `§` names a requirement number **in the target's own numbering**, so both sides of a
  relationship read the same numbers. Ranges are `§3-7`; single requirements are `§4`.

### The Decision log

Every PRD that anything changes or supersedes carries a `## Decision log` as its last section. Entries
are appended in date order, oldest first, one per decision:

```
## Decision log

- **2026-09-14 — PRD 30 §17-19.** Progress now reads in questions answered against an approximate
  denominator, replacing "6 of your goal's ideas settled". A count that can legitimately sit at zero
  through a dozen thoughtful answers reads as no progress, and pre-assessment only works while the
  learner stays willing to answer honestly. Rewrites §18 and the Learner experience status line.
```

Each entry names the date, the PRD and requirements that drove the change, what changed, **why**, and
which of this PRD's own requirements moved. The why is the part that cannot be recovered from a diff,
so never omit it. An entry is never edited once written; a later reversal is a new entry.

### Where things must not go

- Do not write an amendment as a prose section, a bold inline paragraph, or a dated sentence inside a
  bullet. Those four notations all existed at once and none of them were checkable. The header fields
  and the Decision log are the only places.
- Do not record a relationship only in the README. The README index is generated by
  `m sync-prd-links` from the files.
- Do not put a plain defect in a PRD. A bug that needs no product decision belongs in
  `docs/product/defects.md`.
